<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Saudeleur dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1628 CE (approx.) · capital Nan Madol</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first rulers to unite the island of Pohnpei, remembered for the basalt city of Nan Madol, built on artificial islets in a coral lagoon.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Pohnpeian oral tradition says two sorcerer brothers, Olisihpa and Olosohpa, came from overseas and built an altar on the reef off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temwen-island">Temwen Island</a>, the start of Nan Madol. Olosohpa became the first Saudeleur, meaning lord of Deleur, and his line brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pohnpei">Pohnpei's</a> chiefdoms under one ruler, probably around 1100 to 1200.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nan Madol, nearly 100 islets of stacked basalt columns and coral fill, served as royal residence, ceremonial centre and burial ground for the Saudeleur. The rulers demanded heavy tribute in food and labour, and tradition remembers them as increasingly harsh and controlling. Priests tended sacred eels and turtles, and the island's population lived from breadfruit, yams and fishing. The dynasty's history is known almost entirely from oral tradition and archaeology.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Tradition says the hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isokelekel">Isokelekel</a>, son of the thunder god, came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosrae">Kosrae</a> with warriors and overthrew the last Saudeleur, traditionally around 1628. He founded the Nahnmwarki system of chiefdoms that still survives on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pohnpei">Pohnpei</a>. Nan Madol was gradually abandoned and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2016.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/saudeleur-dynasty.jpg" alt="Saudeleur dynasty" width="320" height="314"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Aotearoa assumed (based on copyright claims). · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pohnpei_Island.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 CE – 1628 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nan Madol</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Pohnpeian traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pohnpeian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/micronesia">Micronesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Saudeleur, Nan Madol</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1364&amp;focus=saudeleur-dynasty" aria-label="Saudeleur dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="9063 1922 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Saudeleur dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M9383 2178h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M9383 2178h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nan Madol. <a href="/atlas?year=1364&amp;focus=saudeleur-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

